//@version=6
// BTC ATTACK 38 — ATTACK 37'S FILTER STACK, TERM 1: EMA200 TREND GATE.
// Queue item 1 from the board: Attack 37 (liquidity sweep reversal) cleared PF 1.0 on BOTH halves
// (1.02423271 / 322 trades, 1.01155847 / 196 trades) for the first time on a provable sample, and
// earned a filter stack -- one term at a time, re-split on every addition, keep only under RATCHET v2.
// This is term 1. Base is byte-identical to attack37-liquidity-sweep-reversal.pine except for the
// single added gate below.
//
// THE ONE CHANGE: require close > EMA200 (long-only regime gate) at the moment of entry.
//
// WHAT IT CLAIMS TO EXPLOIT / WHY THIS TERM, NOT ANOTHER ONE: the board's own queue said aim the next
// filter at the STREAKINESS of the losing stretches (a regime or session gate), not at R -- R is
// already close to the floor this construction allows (LESSON 3 audit below). A failed breakdown
// that reclaims a swing low is the same chart pattern whether the broader trend is up or down, but
// the two contexts are not the same trade: in an uptrend a failed breakdown is a dip getting bought
// by the dominant flow; in a downtrend it is a single failed rescue attempt inside a market that is
// still net supplying, and the mandate's own cross-lab finding (HARD LESSON 32, both user labs) is
// that a fade/reversal construction pointed against the prevailing regime is exactly the situation a
// bias/trend gate is needed for, discovered there only after a long-only version had concealed it for
// months. Attack 37 is long-only and has never been regime-gated -- this tests whether that same
// concealment is happening here.
//
// AUDIT, one line per leg (LESSON 6 -- single leg, long only, unchanged from Attack 37):
//   R >= 0.8% (LESSON 3)      — unchanged from Attack 37, enforced by EXCLUSION, not clamping.
//   Stop beyond STRUCTURE (LESSON 5) — unchanged: sweep bar's own low, strictly below the reclaimed
//                                 swing-low signal level.
//   BINDING (E17)             — the new term is trendOk = close > ema200. Reported via trade count:
//                                 if it removes few trades, it is inert (HARD LESSON 12/18 apply); if
//                                 it removes many, read it as regime-dependent, not "harmful" or
//                                 "helpful" from the count alone.
//   REDUNDANCY (E14)          — trendOk (a moving-average trend read) is not a proxy for sweep or
//                                 reclaim (both intrabar structural price comparisons); it is a new,
//                                 independent axis (slow trend vs. fast structural failure).
//   LATCH IN SEQUENCE (LESSON 8) — unchanged: swingLow uses [1] shift, trigger fires on a later bar.
//                                 ema200 is a running series with no lookahead; trendOk is checked on
//                                 the same entry bar as the reclaim, which is legitimate (it is a
//                                 state read, not a second latch that needs its own sequencing).
//   SL and TP FIXED AT ENTRY. No trailing, no averaging down, no martingale. Unchanged.
//
// OUTCOMES REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN (LESSON 17):
//   * PF improves on BOTH halves, materially (not noise-scale) -> KEEP under RATCHET v2 clause 1;
//     check clauses 2 (DD band) and 3 (30-trade floor) before calling it kept.
//   * PF improves on one half and worsens on the other -> REJECTED per the board's own instruction:
//     "a term that improves one half and hurts the other is rejected, not averaged." Not eligible for
//     a tolerance band regardless of magnitude.
//   * PF worsens or is flat on both -> REVERTED, recorded in the tried-and-reverted table, and the
//     next filter-stack term should target something other than trend (a session/time-of-day gate is
//     the other candidate the queue named).
//   * trade count falls by more than 50% on either half -> a split test is already what this cycle IS
//     (both halves independently re-run), so RATCHET v2 clause 4 is satisfied by construction; still
//     report the removal count explicitly per HARD LESSON 12/18.
strategy("BTC Attack38 sweep + EMA200 trend gate", overlay=true,
     pyramiding=1, process_orders_on_close=true,
     commission_type=strategy.commission.percent, commission_value=0.05,
     initial_capital=10000,
     default_qty_type=strategy.percent_of_equity, default_qty_value=100,
     margin_long=100, margin_short=100)

lookback = input.int(20,     "Swing-low lookback, bars")
rr       = input.float(2.0,  "Reward:risk")
minRpct  = input.float(0.80, "Min stop distance as % of price (LESSON 3)")
maxBars  = input.int(192,    "Max bars in trade -- 2 days on 15m")
trendLen = input.int(200,    "EMA trend length (NEW TERM)")

ema200 = ta.ema(close, trendLen)
trendOk = close > ema200

swingLow = ta.lowest(low, lookback)[1]

sweep   = not na(swingLow) and low < swingLow
reclaim = not na(swingLow) and close > swingLow
setupOn = sweep and reclaim

minR  = close * minRpct / 100
rLong = close - low
rBig  = rLong >= minR

goLong = setupOn and rBig and trendOk

var float slPx   = na
var float tpPx   = na
var int   barsIn = 0

flat = strategy.position_size == 0

if flat and goLong and rLong > 0
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
    slPx   := low
    tpPx   := close + rLong * rr
    barsIn := 0

if strategy.position_size > 0
    strategy.exit("LX", from_entry="L", stop=slPx, limit=tpPx)
    barsIn := barsIn + 1

if barsIn >= maxBars and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all()
    barsIn := 0

plot(goLong ? 1 : 0, title="LongSignal")
plot(swingLow,       title="SwingLow")
plot(ema200,         title="EMA200")
